1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,800
You buy a brand-new drive.

2
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:04,600
One terabyte, completely empty.

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:08,630
Before it will hold a single file,
it first has to be formatted.

4
00:00:09,101 --> 00:00:14,421
So you format it with ext4, the standard
filesystem on many Linux computers.

5
00:00:15,601 --> 00:00:18,921
With the default settings,
before you've saved anything at all,

6
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,221
about seventeen gigabytes are gone.

7
00:00:21,807 --> 00:00:23,727
Nothing has been stored yet.

8
00:00:24,187 --> 00:00:26,087
No photos, no documents.

9
00:00:26,487 --> 00:00:28,367
So what's taking up all that space?

10
00:00:28,939 --> 00:00:32,059
And why does a drive need formatting
in the first place?

11
00:00:32,619 --> 00:00:34,219
It can already store data.

12
00:00:34,899 --> 00:00:37,199
It can read and write trillions of bits.

13
00:00:37,957 --> 00:00:41,557
Why can't the computer just start writing
wherever there's room?

14
00:00:42,127 --> 00:00:45,947
Because storing data and finding it again
are two very different problems.

15
00:00:48,617 --> 00:00:52,117
Imagine an office that hands out
blank paper instead of forms.

16
00:00:52,477 --> 00:00:54,917
Everything gets written down somewhere...

17
00:00:55,017 --> 00:00:56,957
and nobody can find anything.

18
00:00:57,326 --> 00:01:00,446
A drive without a filesystem
is that pile of paper.

19
00:01:01,266 --> 00:01:03,566
A better picture, though, is a notebook.

20
00:01:04,336 --> 00:01:08,856
Picture a notebook with a thousand pages,
and a few rules for using it.

21
00:01:09,381 --> 00:01:11,461
First, every page has a number.

22
00:01:12,041 --> 00:01:14,581
Now every spot in the notebook
has an address.

23
00:01:15,381 --> 00:01:18,661
Page four hundred and twelve
means exactly one place.

24
00:01:19,337 --> 00:01:22,337
Second, the first few pages
are a table of contents.

25
00:01:23,137 --> 00:01:25,997
Each entry gives a name,
and the page where it lives.

26
00:01:26,637 --> 00:01:28,457
Pancakes: page twelve.

27
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:33,140
Third, at the very back, there's a list
of the pages that are still blank.

28
00:01:33,848 --> 00:01:35,328
Now add a recipe.

29
00:01:35,948 --> 00:01:37,848
You check the back for a blank page.

30
00:01:38,248 --> 00:01:39,928
You write the recipe there.

31
00:01:40,148 --> 00:01:41,848
You add a line to the contents.

32
00:01:42,648 --> 00:01:44,888
And you cross that page off
the blank list.

33
00:01:45,557 --> 00:01:47,517
One recipe, three places.

34
00:01:47,597 --> 00:01:49,777
And all three have to agree.

35
00:01:50,455 --> 00:01:52,475
Then the pancake recipe grows.

36
00:01:53,235 --> 00:01:56,795
Page twelve is full,
and page thirteen is already taken.

37
00:01:57,495 --> 00:02:01,595
So it carries on at page eighty-seven,
and the contents has to say so.

38
00:02:02,097 --> 00:02:03,437
And here's the catch.

39
00:02:04,257 --> 00:02:07,637
If you want room for a thousand entries
in the table of contents,

40
00:02:07,897 --> 00:02:11,777
you have to set those pages aside
before you write a single recipe.

41
00:02:14,377 --> 00:02:18,017
That's the analogy.
Here's what's actually on the drive.

42
00:02:19,227 --> 00:02:22,627
A drive doesn't know about files,
folders or names.

43
00:02:23,527 --> 00:02:26,947
All it offers is a long row
of numbered slots, called sectors.

44
00:02:27,730 --> 00:02:30,870
On a hard drive,
the data sits on spinning platters.

45
00:02:31,590 --> 00:02:34,070
An SSD keeps it in flash memory.

46
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:37,050
Either way, the computer only ever asks

47
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:41,310
for a sector by its number,
and the drive works out where that is.

48
00:02:41,910 --> 00:02:48,930
A sector is small. Usually 512 bytes,
or 4,096 on newer drives.

49
00:02:49,688 --> 00:02:52,928
Filesystems usually work
in bigger pieces, called blocks.

50
00:02:53,928 --> 00:02:59,188
On ext4, a block is four kilobytes,
the size of eight old-style sectors.

51
00:02:59,919 --> 00:03:02,479
Those numbered blocks
are the notebook's pages.

52
00:03:03,379 --> 00:03:06,419
What the filesystem still needs
are the other two parts:

53
00:03:06,659 --> 00:03:09,319
a table of contents,
and a list of what's free.

54
00:03:09,937 --> 00:03:11,797
That's what formatting builds.

55
00:03:12,337 --> 00:03:14,937
And that's where
those seventeen gigabytes went.

56
00:03:16,067 --> 00:03:19,667
Ext4 sets aside a record
for every file and folder

57
00:03:19,897 --> 00:03:22,227
the drive will be able to hold at once.

58
00:03:23,107 --> 00:03:27,747
Each record is 256 bytes.
It's called an inode.

59
00:03:28,733 --> 00:03:32,653
One day, each inode will say
how big a file is, when it was changed,

60
00:03:32,893 --> 00:03:35,853
who may open it,
and which blocks hold its contents.

61
00:03:36,829 --> 00:03:41,789
On a one terabyte drive, ext4 prepares
about sixty-one million of them.

62
00:03:42,329 --> 00:03:44,229
All empty, all waiting.

63
00:03:44,989 --> 00:03:47,869
That's almost sixteen gigabytes
of blank records.

64
00:03:48,619 --> 00:03:49,599
Why sixty-one million?

65
00:03:49,829 --> 00:03:53,259
Ext4 makes a simple guess:

66
00:03:53,359 --> 00:03:57,079
one inode for every sixteen kilobytes
of space on the drive.

67
00:03:57,779 --> 00:04:01,799
On a typical computer,
most of those records will never be used.

68
00:04:02,415 --> 00:04:05,955
About one more gigabyte goes to a journal,
which we'll come back to.

69
00:04:06,775 --> 00:04:10,675
The rest is other bookkeeping,
including a map with one bit per block,

70
00:04:10,755 --> 00:04:13,015
saying whether it's used or free.

71
00:04:13,449 --> 00:04:15,169
That number is fixed, too.

72
00:04:15,749 --> 00:04:18,089
Use up every record
with millions of tiny files,

73
00:04:18,319 --> 00:04:20,569
and the drive will tell you it's full...

74
00:04:20,749 --> 00:04:23,029
even with gigabytes of space left.

75
00:04:25,639 --> 00:04:27,179
So let's watch it work.

76
00:04:27,599 --> 00:04:30,139
We'll create a file called hello.txt.

77
00:04:30,719 --> 00:04:35,479
Inside, just one word: hello.
Five letters. Five bytes.

78
00:04:36,237 --> 00:04:38,967
The filesystem picks an empty inode.

79
00:04:39,277 --> 00:04:40,977
Let's say number thirteen.

80
00:04:41,657 --> 00:04:44,677
Then it finds a free block in the map,
and marks it as used.

81
00:04:45,361 --> 00:04:47,721
It writes the five bytes into that block.

82
00:04:48,501 --> 00:04:51,081
Then it fills in the inode:
five bytes long,

83
00:04:51,311 --> 00:04:54,101
created just now,
and stored in that one block.

84
00:04:54,439 --> 00:04:57,199
And finally, in the folder,
it adds one line:

85
00:04:57,459 --> 00:05:00,619
the name hello.txt,
and the number thirteen.

86
00:05:01,255 --> 00:05:04,855
Notice where the name ended up.
Not in the file.

87
00:05:05,255 --> 00:05:06,795
Not in the inode.

88
00:05:06,955 --> 00:05:11,135
The name lives in the folder, which is
really just a list of names and numbers.

89
00:05:11,609 --> 00:05:13,929
And a folder is just another file.

90
00:05:14,489 --> 00:05:16,869
What it happens to contain is that list.

91
00:05:17,574 --> 00:05:18,954
That's also why moving a file

92
00:05:19,184 --> 00:05:21,074
to another folder on the same filesystem

93
00:05:21,154 --> 00:05:23,894
is instant, however big it is.

94
00:05:24,474 --> 00:05:27,994
One folder loses a line,
the other gains one,

95
00:05:28,074 --> 00:05:30,554
and usually, the contents
don't move at all.

96
00:05:31,091 --> 00:05:35,881
Five bytes of text...
and on the drive, it takes up 4,096.

97
00:05:36,651 --> 00:05:40,031
A block is the smallest piece
the filesystem hands out,

98
00:05:40,111 --> 00:05:42,851
so even a tiny file costs a whole one.

99
00:05:43,370 --> 00:05:47,490
When the file outgrows its block,
the filesystem finds another free one.

100
00:05:47,910 --> 00:05:49,690
Maybe right next door.

101
00:05:49,850 --> 00:05:51,710
Maybe somewhere else entirely.

102
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:54,350
And the inode keeps track of both.

103
00:05:55,033 --> 00:05:58,753
Over time, a big file can end up
in pieces all over the drive.

104
00:05:59,213 --> 00:06:02,053
A hard drive has to jump between them.

105
00:06:02,593 --> 00:06:04,913
An SSD copes with that much better.

106
00:06:05,608 --> 00:06:08,428
The program that saved the file
knows none of this.

107
00:06:09,128 --> 00:06:13,728
It said "save hello.txt",
and everything else happened underneath.

108
00:06:14,474 --> 00:06:15,414
Now delete it.

109
00:06:15,494 --> 00:06:17,414
The name is removed from the folder.

110
00:06:18,074 --> 00:06:19,974
The inode is marked as free.

111
00:06:20,494 --> 00:06:22,494
The block goes back on the free list.

112
00:06:23,171 --> 00:06:27,071
On a traditional hard drive,
the five bytes themselves stay put.

113
00:06:27,451 --> 00:06:29,331
Nobody touched them.

114
00:06:29,791 --> 00:06:32,551
They remain until something new
is written on top.

115
00:06:33,213 --> 00:06:36,633
That's why a deleted file
can sometimes be recovered.

116
00:06:37,313 --> 00:06:39,993
Deleting usually doesn't erase
the contents.

117
00:06:40,753 --> 00:06:43,013
It just marks the space as available.

118
00:06:43,766 --> 00:06:46,186
On an SSD, it's less certain.

119
00:06:46,866 --> 00:06:50,086
The filesystem can tell the drive
that those blocks are no longer needed,

120
00:06:50,316 --> 00:06:53,086
and the drive may discard what's in them.

121
00:06:53,486 --> 00:06:57,146
After that, getting the file back
is much less likely.

122
00:06:57,733 --> 00:07:00,213
So a filesystem is really a bookkeeper.

123
00:07:00,973 --> 00:07:03,973
Creating this one file
meant updating three things:

124
00:07:04,073 --> 00:07:06,753
the folder, the inode,
and the map of free blocks.

125
00:07:09,623 --> 00:07:12,443
Now cut the power
halfway through saving a file.

126
00:07:13,511 --> 00:07:17,371
Maybe the block was marked as used,
but no inode points to it yet.

127
00:07:17,971 --> 00:07:21,831
That space is simply lost.
Annoying, but harmless.

128
00:07:22,356 --> 00:07:26,316
Or maybe the inode points to a block
that the map still calls free.

129
00:07:27,136 --> 00:07:29,616
The next file can be written
right on top of it.

130
00:07:29,696 --> 00:07:32,456
And the first one is quietly destroyed.

131
00:07:33,272 --> 00:07:35,912
That's why ext4 keeps a journal.

132
00:07:36,532 --> 00:07:40,532
Before it changes its bookkeeping,
it writes down what it's about to do.

133
00:07:41,172 --> 00:07:43,272
After a crash, it replays only the changes

134
00:07:43,502 --> 00:07:46,592
it had finished writing down,
and marked as complete.

135
00:07:47,432 --> 00:07:49,472
Anything unfinished is ignored.

136
00:07:51,212 --> 00:07:52,972
And there's a quieter problem.

137
00:07:53,552 --> 00:07:57,032
Sometimes a drive changes a bit
and doesn't tell anyone.

138
00:07:57,582 --> 00:08:02,562
Ext4 protects its own records with
checksums, so it can spot damage there.

139
00:08:03,282 --> 00:08:04,962
But the contents of your files?

140
00:08:05,622 --> 00:08:07,222
Those, it simply trusts.

141
00:08:08,050 --> 00:08:10,190
Some filesystems check both.

142
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:13,490
And some keep a second copy,
so when one copy is damaged,

143
00:08:13,720 --> 00:08:15,830
they can repair it from the other.

144
00:08:16,590 --> 00:08:19,630
Spotting a problem and fixing it
are not the same thing.

145
00:08:22,260 --> 00:08:24,880
So every filesystem does the same job:

146
00:08:25,020 --> 00:08:28,160
numbered blocks, a list of names,
and a map of what's free.

147
00:08:28,577 --> 00:08:30,017
But the choices differ.

148
00:08:30,977 --> 00:08:35,137
Ext4 prepares sixty-one million records
before you save a thing.

149
00:08:35,997 --> 00:08:41,037
NTFS, on Windows, keeps its records
in one big table that grows as you go.

150
00:08:41,448 --> 00:08:45,388
And Btrfs doesn't prepare
a fixed table of inodes in advance.

151
00:08:46,248 --> 00:08:48,348
It creates them as files arrive.

152
00:08:49,208 --> 00:08:52,008
And normally, it doesn't overwrite
data in place.

153
00:08:52,568 --> 00:08:56,388
It writes a new copy,
and only then lets go of the old one.

154
00:08:56,910 --> 00:09:01,870
By default, it also checksums your files,
so it can tell when they've been damaged.

155
00:09:02,423 --> 00:09:06,603
And because the old copy isn't
overwritten, a snapshot can keep it alive.

156
00:09:07,363 --> 00:09:10,843
That changes the answer
to almost every question in this video:

157
00:09:10,983 --> 00:09:14,763
what a crash does, what deleting means,
and even what a copy is.

158
00:09:17,223 --> 00:09:19,143
That's where the next video begins.

159
00:09:20,963 --> 00:09:22,823
So here's a question for you.

160
00:09:23,323 --> 00:09:27,183
Should a filesystem just store your files,
or should it protect them too?

161
00:09:27,723 --> 00:09:28,973
Let me know in the comments.
